Subject: mountmgr.sys: Drive letters as Windows gives them, in MountedDevices.

Patch 0102 kept a letter for every volume it had ever seen, and a volume was
its MBR signature: a program writing a stick (Rufus writes a new signature each
time) made a new disk of it, and the letters climbed towards Z:.

MountedDevices now is what it is in Windows, a value for each letter holding the
volume that owns it, kept from one start to the next: a partition by "DMIO:ID:"
and its GPT GUID or by its MBR signature and offset, a USB stick by its USBSTOR
device, as Windows 10 has them. A volume comes back to the letter it owns if
that is free, else the first free one from D:. A stick owns none when it is
gone unless it was given one on purpose. A fixed disk's letter stays its own
while it is away and no other volume takes it, which is more than Windows does:
that is 0102's reason, a stick that came first took D: and moved every disk.

A volume has one letter: IOCTL_MOUNTMGR_CREATE_POINT refuses a second, and
while the volume is being mounted for it the letter asked for is the one it
gets (Rufus's stick had N: from the mount and G: from Rufus). A partition of a
type Windows knows has its letter with no file system on it yet.
